As team member two, my task was to implement a file that downloads and collates the module.
To do this, I created a function that downloaded the files and saved them locally onto my computer.
This allowed me to avoid manually downloading each file individually, making the process more efficient.

I then made another function that combined all the files into one collated file.
This meant all my resources were in the same location, making them easier for the rest of the team to access.

Throughout the process, I used Github to commit and manage my code so that my work would be updated for my group.
This allowed for more effective collaboration on my side.
In addition, I tested the program using Spyder to ensure that the downloading process was correct, with files combining with no errors.

One challenge that I did face was locating the collated file after running the code in Spyder. 
It was difficult to find where the generated file had been saved on my computer.
To overcome this, I used Spyder to list all the files within the folder directory and ran additional code to identify where the downloaded files were being stored.
Through doing this, I was able to correctly locate the collated file and improve my understanding of file paths.

Although my main role was preparing and collating the data rather than carrying out the final analysis pipeline, my work contributed towards enabling pattern detection within the project.
After collating the files into a single dataset, the positions of the correct answers will be more easily reviewed and analysed for possible patterns.
From my own review of the collated data, I did not carry out sufficient statistical analysis to conclusively determine whether a deliberate pattern existed.
This task helped me improve my understanding of file handling, debugging and version control using Github.
